            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is a Task?</h2>
                    <p>Tasks represent asynchronous operations; a <code>Task</code> doesn't return a value, while <code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code> does - await them to get results.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Task - no return value
Task task = DoSomethingAsync();

// Task&lt;T&gt; - returns a value
Task&lt;string&gt; taskWithResult = GetDataAsync();
string result = await taskWithResult;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Task States</h2>
                    <p>Inspect task state and status properties to diagnose or monitor task lifecycle (running, completed, faulted, canceled).</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">var task = DoWorkAsync();

Console.WriteLine(task.Status);        // Running, WaitingForActivation, etc.
Console.WriteLine(task.IsCompleted);   // true when finished (success, fault, or cancel)
Console.WriteLine(task.IsCompletedSuccessfully);  // true only on success
Console.WriteLine(task.IsFaulted);     // true if exception thrown
Console.WriteLine(task.IsCanceled);    // true if canceled</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Creating Tasks</h2>
                    <p>Different ways to create tasks: completed tasks, Task.Run for CPU-bound work, and wrapping async lambdas for background operations.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// 1. Return completed task
Task&lt;int&gt; completed = Task.FromResult(42);

// 2. Return completed Task (no value)
Task done = Task.CompletedTask;

// 3. Run CPU-bound work on thread pool
Task cpuWork = Task.Run(() =&gt; 
{
    // CPU-intensive work here
    return ComputeValue();
});

// 4. Create from async lambda
Task asyncWork = Task.Run(async () =&gt;
{
    await Task.Delay(100);
    return "result";
});</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Task vs Thread</h2>
                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Task</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>High-level abstraction</li>
                                <li>Uses thread pool</li>
                                <li>Supports async/await</li>
                                <li>Composable</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Thread</h4>
                            <ul>
                                <li>Low-level primitive</li>
                                <li>Expensive to create</li>
                                <li>Manual management</li>
                                <li>Rarely needed directly</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Task represents an async operation.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Task&lt;T&gt; returns a value, Task does not.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use Task.Run for CPU-bound work.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Prefer Task over Thread for most scenarios.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
